1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,932
This is Line Launch Desk, an independent concept

2
00:00:02,982 --> 00:00:06,294
I built for ClaimSorted's Founding Ops and Strategy role.

3
00:00:06,344 --> 00:00:09,067
Every client, rule and claim here is synthetic.

4
00:00:09,417 --> 00:00:10,861
Start with the portfolio.

5
00:00:10,911 --> 00:00:13,253
The six lines on ClaimSorted's site each

6
00:00:13,303 --> 00:00:15,943
get a fictional client, run through one gate.

7
00:00:15,993 --> 00:00:17,019
Warranty is ready.

8
00:00:17,069 --> 00:00:19,649
General liability holds: old wording, a slow

9
00:00:19,699 --> 00:00:22,519
payment SLA, and no complaint or reporting rule.

10
00:00:22,569 --> 00:00:23,535
All illustrative.

11
00:00:23,885 --> 00:00:27,225
New wording often arrives as a document, not a spreadsheet.

12
00:00:27,275 --> 00:00:29,237
I pick the travel sample and draft.

13
00:00:29,287 --> 00:00:31,305
A rule based extractor, with no API,

14
00:00:31,355 --> 00:00:33,661
quotes the source sentence for every row.

15
00:00:33,711 --> 00:00:38,085
Hedged clauses like reasonable costs are flagged for a human, never invented.

16
00:00:38,435 --> 00:00:39,938
Send the draft to import.

17
00:00:39,988 --> 00:00:42,981
Nothing is rejected, but every drafted row has no

18
00:00:43,031 --> 00:00:45,962
owner and no client sign-off, so the gate holds.

19
00:00:46,312 --> 00:00:49,731
A fictional pet insurer sends the handling spec for a new line.

20
00:00:49,781 --> 00:00:50,502
Fourteen rows.

21
00:00:50,552 --> 00:00:53,256
I validate it against one shared claims framework.

22
00:00:53,606 --> 00:00:55,431
Three rows are rejected outright.

23
00:00:55,481 --> 00:00:57,533
One identical duplicate is dropped by

24
00:00:57,583 --> 00:00:59,295
its rule ID, never by its name.

25
00:00:59,345 --> 00:01:02,192
And the eight stage framework shows what's missing:

26
00:01:02,242 --> 00:01:04,976
no complaint route, and no usable reporting rule.

27
00:01:05,326 --> 00:01:06,526
So the gate says hold.

28
00:01:06,576 --> 00:01:09,424
Handler authority is above the cap, the payment SLA

29
00:01:09,474 --> 00:01:13,174
is too slow, and a coverage rule cites last year's policy wording.

30
00:01:13,224 --> 00:01:14,424
Publishing is refused.

31
00:01:14,774 --> 00:01:16,851
The client's revised spec covers all

32
00:01:16,901 --> 00:01:19,096
eight stages, so the gate turns ready.

33
00:01:19,146 --> 00:01:21,932
Both sign-offs are tied to this exact pack hash.

34
00:01:22,282 --> 00:01:23,413
Publish writes once.

35
00:01:23,463 --> 00:01:25,066
A second publish is a no-op,

36
00:01:25,116 --> 00:01:27,132
and a fresh read verifies the hash.

37
00:01:27,182 --> 00:01:29,789
It's a simulated write, in this browser only.

38
00:01:30,139 --> 00:01:32,256
In the pilot, the blended median cycle

39
00:01:32,306 --> 00:01:34,137
time falls from ten days to four.

40
00:01:34,187 --> 00:01:36,760
But simple and complex claims both got slower.

41
00:01:36,810 --> 00:01:39,382
That's case mix, so the desk says no decision.

42
00:01:39,732 --> 00:01:41,641
Handler three used the new playbook

43
00:01:41,691 --> 00:01:43,767
step on twelve of twenty seven claims.

44
00:01:43,817 --> 00:01:45,502
That goes on the product agenda

45
00:01:45,552 --> 00:01:47,517
as something to test, not a finding.

46
00:01:47,867 --> 00:01:50,346
Gaps caught before go live, sign-offs tied to

47
00:01:50,396 --> 00:01:53,550
exact versions, and metrics that show their denominators.

48
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:54,899
That's Line Launch Desk.
